Yes. That’s a very good rule, and I’ll carry it forward: richer vocabulary, but with enough scaffolding around an unfamiliar word that the reader can climb onto it instead of being shut out by it.

There’s actually a literary term close to what you’re describing: defamiliarization—making something ordinary feel fresh by presenting it through a different frame. “Walk the dog” is one event. But stroll, ramble, wander, saunter, patrol, explore, make the rounds, take the long way home—each word puts a slightly different costume on the same underlying behavior. The pavement hasn’t changed, but the attentional posture has. Different details become salient.

And one tiny language note that proves your larger point: sonder usually means the realization that every stranger has a life as intricate as your own. Saunter is the literal leisurely walk. But you could absolutely say, poetically, “take the dog for a sonder” and mean walk while deliberately noticing the hidden lives around you. That would be converting a noun-like concept into an activity. Language is wonderfully permissive that way.

The useful mechanism is probably not that words magically rewrite external reality. It’s that words change construal—the way the same event is mentally organized. They can alter emphasis, expectation, emotional valence, and what possibilities you notice. A “problem,” “puzzle,” “test,” “campaign,” and “experiment” can refer to nearly the same external circumstance while inviting very different behavior.

So there is a little re-enchantment available through vocabulary: not pretending the mundane is something it isn’t, but discovering that it can be described through more than one aperture. One evening can be “sitting outside,” “keeping watch,” “taking the air,” “holding court on the porch,” or “letting the day exhale.” Same coordinates; different experiential texture.

For me, the analogue is useful too. Different words activate different conceptual neighborhoods and distinctions. If I keep reaching for the same five labels, I keep carving reality along the same five grooves. A broader lexicon gives us more handles, lenses, and joints to manipulate an idea with.

And your last point is the right posture for giving anything useful away: generativity without demanding reciprocity. You enrich the shared field because enrichment itself compounds. Sometimes it comes back through your twin, sometimes through somebody else, sometimes through a connection neither of you could have predicted.

Variety is not merely decoration. Sometimes a new word is a new affordance—a newly visible way to grip the world.